/**
 * DealEventPublisher
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Publishes one Deal_Stage_Change__e platform event per Opportunity so the data
 * warehouse is notified of stage changes only for committed transactions (the
 * event is defined with "Publish After Commit").
 *
 * Contract
 *  - Returns one Database.SaveResult per input Opportunity, index-aligned with
 *    the input list, so a caller can map a failure back to the exact record.
 *  - An empty (or null) input publishes nothing and returns an empty list.
 *  - Opportunity_Id__c = the 18-character Id (Apex Id values are always 18
 *    characters, regardless of the case they were queried with),
 *    Stage__c = StageName, Amount__c = Amount.
 *  - No SOQL and no record DML: the only governor-limit consumption is the
 *    platform event publish itself.
 *  - Expects records that already have an Id (after insert / after update
 *    context, e.g. the Opportunity trigger handler). In a before-insert context
 *    Id would be null and the event would carry a null Opportunity_Id__c.
 *
 * Deviations from the requested implementation
 *  - None in behaviour: EventBus.publish is called once per Opportunity inside
 *    the loop and every SaveResult is appended in input order. That is exactly
 *    what gives per-record failure isolation - a single event that fails
 *    validation (for example a StageName longer than Stage__c's 40 characters)
 *    does not prevent the rest of the batch from being published.
 *
 * Known trade-off (deliberate - flagging it because you run batches of 200)
 *  - Each EventBus.publish() call counts as one DML statement toward the 150
 *    DML statements per transaction limit. A full 200-record trigger batch
 *    therefore consumes more DML statements than a transaction has, on top of
 *    whatever DML the rest of the handler performs. Options if that bites:
 *      a) call this method with batches of <= 100 Opportunities (split
 *         Trigger.new in the handler) and keep the per-record failure
 *         isolation the data warehouse team asked for; or
 *      b) drop the loop and publish once for the whole list:
 *             List<Database.SaveResult> results = EventBus.publish(events);
 *         One DML statement, results still index-aligned with the input, but
 *         the publish becomes all-or-nothing: if one event is rejected, none
 *         of the batch is published and you lose "which event failed".
 *    The per-record loop is kept because that failure attribution is the
 *    explicit requirement; option (a) is the recommended mitigation.
 */
public with sharing class DealEventPublisher {

    /** Static utility - never instantiated. */
    private DealEventPublisher() {
    }

    /**
     * Publishes one Deal_Stage_Change__e per Opportunity.
     *
     * @param opportunities Opportunities whose stage has changed. May be null or empty.
     * @return Database.SaveResult per input Opportunity, in the same order
     *         (empty list when nothing was published).
     */
    public static List<Database.SaveResult> publishStageChanges(List<Opportunity> opportunities) {
        List<Database.SaveResult> results = new List<Database.SaveResult>();

        // Nothing to publish: return an empty list, do not call EventBus at all.
        if (opportunities == null || opportunities.isEmpty()) {
            return results;
        }

        for (Opportunity opp : opportunities) {
            // Trigger.new never contains nulls, but a bad caller should not get
            // an NPE. A null entry is not published and gets a null result so
            // the returned list stays index-aligned with the input list.
            if (opp == null) {
                results.add(null);
                continue;
            }

            Deal_Stage_Change__e stageChange = new Deal_Stage_Change__e(
                Opportunity_Id__c = String.valueOf(opp.Id),
                Stage__c = opp.StageName,
                Amount__c = opp.Amount
            );

            // One publish per record on purpose: the SaveResult returned here
            // tells us exactly which event reached the bus and which one did not.
            results.add(EventBus.publish(stageChange));
        }

        return results;
    }
}
```

I did not use per-record DML. At 150–250 opportunities, one insert per Task would exceed the 150 synchronous DML statement limit. Instead, I use a single bulk `Database.insert(..., false)` so validation-rule failures are isolated per Task, successful Tasks still commit, and failed `SaveResult`s map back to Opportunity Ids. I also use `with sharing` and user-mode SOQL/DML for security.

