public with sharing class LeadContactMatcher {
    public static List<Id> matchByEmail(List<Lead> leads, List<Contact> contacts) {
        List<Id> matchedContactIds = new List<Id>();

        if (leads == null) {
            return matchedContactIds;
        }

        Map<String, Id> firstContactIdByEmail = new Map<String, Id>();

        if (contacts != null) {
            for (Contact c : contacts) {
                if (c == null) {
                    continue;
                }

                String emailKey = normalizeEmail(c.Email);
                if (emailKey == null) {
                    continue;
                }

                if (!firstContactIdByEmail.containsKey(emailKey)) {
                    firstContactIdByEmail.put(emailKey, c.Id);
                }
            }
        }

        for (Lead lead : leads) {
            Id matchedContactId = null;

            if (lead != null) {
                String emailKey = normalizeEmail(lead.Email);
                if (emailKey != null) {
                    matchedContactId = firstContactIdByEmail.get(emailKey);
                }
            }

            matchedContactIds.add(matchedContactId);
        }

        return matchedContactIds;
    }

    private static String normalizeEmail(String email) {
        if (email == null) {
            return null;
        }

        String trimmed = email.trim();
        if (trimmed.length() == 0) {
            return null;
        }

        return trimmed.toLowerCase();
    }
}
```

I would use the normalized-email map rather than the nested loop: 3,000 leads × 3,000 contacts is 9 million comparisons inside the import transaction, which can approach Apex CPU limits. The map keeps the first contact in `contacts` order via `containsKey` and remains read-only, query-free, and DML-free.

